  <header class="mb-8 mt-4">
    <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold mb-3">Planning an Office Lunch in Santee: A Sandwich Catering Checklist</h1>
    <p class="text-zinc-500 text-sm">By Pepper Farm Deli · Published September 15, 2026</p>
  </header>

  <p class="mb-5">A good office lunch does more than fill a table. It gives people a chance to step away from work, makes guests feel welcome, and keeps the day on schedule. Whether you are planning a team meeting, training session, client visit, or casual celebration in Santee, a little preparation makes group ordering much easier.</p>

  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">1. Start with an accurate headcount</h2>
  <p class="mb-5">Confirm how many people will eat before choosing food. Ask attendees to respond by a clear deadline, then build in a small buffer for last-minute guests when your budget allows. A final count helps you order the right quantity and avoids running short or creating unnecessary waste.</p>

  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">2. Ask about dietary needs early</h2>
  <p class="mb-5">Send a quick message before ordering to identify vegetarian preferences, food allergies, gluten-related needs, and ingredient dislikes. Group lunches are easier when there is more than one kind of sandwich or salad available. If someone has an allergy, contact the restaurant directly before ordering so the team can explain current ingredients and preparation options.</p>

  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">3. Choose food that works for the meeting format</h2>
  <p class="mb-5">For a working lunch, handheld options and clearly labeled sides keep setup simple. For a longer gathering, add a mix of sandwiches, salads, wraps, and drinks so people can choose what suits them. Keep serving space, utensils, napkins, and refrigeration in mind if food will sit out before the meeting begins.</p>

  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">4. Set the pickup or delivery time around the agenda</h2>
  <p class="mb-5">Plan for food to arrive shortly before the meal break rather than at the beginning of the event. This helps keep hot food hot and cold food fresh. If your event has a fixed start time, leave room for traffic, building access, and setup. For a pickup order, assign one person to confirm the order and collect it.</p>

  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">5. Make ordering details easy to find</h2>
  <p class="mb-5">Include the event date, desired serving time, headcount, contact name, phone number, and any dietary notes in one place. For offices, it also helps to provide the business name, suite number, parking details, and a point of contact who can meet the order. Clear details reduce follow-up calls and make the day smoother for everyone.</p>

  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">6. Confirm availability before making promises</h2>
  <p class="mb-5">Catering availability, lead times, and menu selections can change. Contact Pepper Farm Deli before finalizing your plans—especially for large groups or short-notice events. The team can help you review current options and choose an order that fits your event.</p>

